+ ## Trust Model & Threat Assumptions
+ **Understanding what MoltGuard can and cannot protect against:**
+ ### What You Must Trust
+ To use MoltGuard, you must trust:
+ 1. **Local sanitization logic** (`moltguard/agent/sanitizer.ts`)
+    - Regex patterns correctly identify and replace all sensitive data
+    - No sensitive data bypasses sanitization before cloud transmission
+    - **Mitigation:** Source code is open and auditable on GitHub
+ 2. **OpenGuardrails Cloud detection service** (for Component #2)
+    - Sanitized metadata is processed and discarded (not stored)
+    - Detection API doesn't log or exfiltrate data beyond stated purpose
+    - **Mitigation:** Only sanitized placeholders sent; verify with network monitoring
+ 3. **Package provenance** (npm package matches GitHub source)
+    - Published package contains expected code without backdoors
+    - **Mitigation:** Compare package contents with GitHub source (see verification steps)
+ 4. **Credential security** (API keys stored locally)
+    - File permissions protect credentials from other users/processes
+    - **Mitigation:** Verify `0600` permissions; monitor file access
+ ### What You Don't Need to Trust
+ MoltGuard does NOT require you to trust:
+ ❌ **Your LLM provider to protect secrets** (Component #1 sanitizes before transmission)
+ ❌ **OpenGuardrails with your LLM provider API keys** (never sent to OpenGuardrails)
+ ❌ **Network intermediaries** (all communication is HTTPS encrypted)
+ ❌ **Other users on your system** (credentials are `0600`, process isolation)
+ ### Threat Model
+ **MoltGuard protects against:**
+ ✅ **Prompt injection attacks** (malicious instructions in files/web content)
+ ✅ **Accidental secret leakage** (API keys in files sent to LLM)
+ ✅ **Social engineering** (tricking agent into dangerous actions)
+ ✅ **Data exfiltration attempts** (hidden commands to send data externally)
+ **MoltGuard does NOT protect against:**
+ ❌ **Compromised OpenClaw installation** (if OpenClaw is backdoored, MoltGuard can't help)
+ ❌ **Malicious npm packages** (if package is compromised, sanitization may be bypassed)
+ ❌ **System-level compromise** (root access, kernel exploits)
+ ❌ **Side-channel attacks** (timing, memory, cache attacks)
+ ❌ **Insider threats** (user with system access can read credential files)
+ ### Security Boundaries
+ **Clear security boundaries:**
+ | Boundary | Protected | Not Protected |
+ |----------|-----------|---------------|
+ | **Agent ↔ LLM Provider** | ✅ Secrets sanitized by gateway | ❌ LLM provider still sees conversation content |
+ | **Agent ↔ OpenGuardrails Cloud** | ✅ Secrets sanitized before detection | ❌ Cloud sees sanitized metadata (tool names, placeholders) |
+ | **Agent ↔ Local Files** | ❌ Agent can read any file it has permissions for | ✅ Secrets sanitized before sending to LLM/cloud |
+ | **Agent ↔ Network** | ✅ Malicious commands detected | ❌ Agent can still make network requests (with warnings) |
+ | **User ↔ Credentials** | ✅ File permissions protect from other users | ❌ User with root can read credential files |
+ ### Assumptions
+ **MoltGuard assumes:**
+ 1. **Your system is not already compromised** (no rootkits, backdoors)
+ 2. **npm package registry is trustworthy** (or you verify package provenance)
+ 3. **Your LLM provider is trustworthy** (to process sanitized data)
+ 4. **OpenClaw itself is trustworthy** (MoltGuard runs inside OpenClaw process)
+ 5. **You verify package contents** (before installing in production)
+ **If any assumption is violated, MoltGuard's security guarantees may not hold.**
+ ### Defense in Depth
+ **MoltGuard is ONE layer of defense. Recommended additional layers:**
+ 1. **System-level sandboxing** (Docker, VM, AppArmor, SELinux)
+ 2. **Network isolation** (firewall, VPN, network policies)
+ 3. **Least privilege** (run OpenClaw with minimal permissions)
+ 4. **Secrets management** (use secret managers, rotate keys regularly)
+ 5. **Audit logging** (monitor all agent actions, network traffic)
+ 6. **Regular updates** (keep MoltGuard, OpenClaw, and system packages updated)
+ **Do not rely solely on MoltGuard for security.**
+ ## ⚠️ SECURITY NOTICE: Read This Before Installing
+ ### DO NOT Install In Production First
+ ❌ **DO NOT install into:**
+ - Production agents with access to sensitive data
+ - Highly privileged environments
+ - Agents with access to production secrets/credentials
+ - Your primary OpenClaw instance (until verified)
+ ✅ **DO install into:**
+ - Isolated VM or container
+ - Test OpenClaw instance with throwaway credentials
+ - Environment you can monitor with network tools
+ - Sandbox without access to production data
+ **See "Testing in a Sandbox" section for step-by-step isolation testing.**
+ ### Understand What Data Leaves Your Machine
+ **MoltGuard has two components with different data flows:**
+ | Component | Where It Runs | Cloud Transmission |
+ |-----------|---------------|-------------------|
+ | **Component #1: AI Security Gateway** | 100% local (`localhost:38790`) | **NONE** — Only talks to YOUR LLM provider |
+ | **Component #2: Security Detection** | Hybrid (local sanitization + cloud API) | **YES** — Sends sanitized metadata to OpenGuardrails Core |
+ - All sanitization processing (happens before cloud transmission)
+ - Sanitized metadata for security detection
+ - Example sent: `curl <URL> -H 'Authorization: Bearer <SECRET>'`
+ ### Credentials Stored Locally
+ MoltGuard creates **two separate credential stores**:
+ 1. **`~/.openclaw/credentials/moltguard/credentials.json`**
+    - Contains: OpenGuardrails detection API key (`sk-og-...`)
+    - Purpose: Authenticate with OpenGuardrails Core for detection
+    - Created during: Auto-registration on first use
+    - Visible via: `/og_status` command
+ 2. **`~/.openguardrails/gateway.json`** (only if you enable gateway)
+    - Contains: YOUR LLM provider API keys (Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini)
+    - Purpose: Local gateway proxy to YOUR provider
+    - Created during: `/og_sanitize on` command
+    - Never sent to OpenGuardrails
+ See "Credentials Storage & Management" section for details.
+ ### Pre-Installation Checklist
+ **Before installing, complete these steps:**
+ - [ ] Read "Architecture Overview" section (understand data flows)
+ - [ ] Read "Security FAQ" section (common concerns answered)
+ - [ ] Verify package provenance: `npm view @openguardrails/moltguard repository.url`
+ - [ ] Review sanitization code on GitHub: `moltguard/agent/sanitizer.ts`
+ - [ ] Set up isolated test environment (VM, container, or test OpenClaw instance)
+ - [ ] Install network monitoring tools (`tcpdump`, `mitmproxy`, or Little Snitch)
+ - [ ] Follow "Testing in a Sandbox" section (complete isolation testing)
+ - [ ] Verify no secrets leak in network traffic during test
+ - [ ] Confirm package contents match GitHub source
+ **Only install in production after:**
+ - ✅ Completing all isolation testing steps
+ - ✅ Verifying network behavior meets your security requirements
+ - ✅ Reviewing and trusting the sanitization logic
+ - ✅ Understanding and accepting the hybrid architecture
+ ### If You Cannot Accept Cloud Transmission
+ MoltGuard requires cloud detection API for threat intelligence. While all sensitive data is sanitized locally before transmission, sanitized metadata (tool names, placeholders) is sent to OpenGuardrails Core.
+ **Alternatives if you need fully local protection:**
+ - Use traditional security tools (AppArmor, SELinux, sandboxing)
+ - Implement custom local guardrails
+ - Wait for a future fully-local version (not currently available)
